/F1 13 Tf
(1. Introduction to Transaction Processing) Tj
0 -16 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(A transaction is a single logical unit of work that accesses and possibly modifies the contents of a database.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(It is a sequence of one or more operations (reads and writes) that must be executed as an atomic unit --) Tj
0 -13 Td
(either all operations complete successfully, or none of them take effect.) Tj
0 -16 Td
/F1 9.5 Tf
(Example: ) Tj
/F2 9.5 Tf
(A bank transfer from Account A to Account B involves two operations: (1) Debit $1000 from Account A,) Tj
0 -13 Td
((2) Credit $1000 to Account B. Both operations together form a single transaction. If only one succeeds, the database) Tj
0 -13 Td
(becomes inconsistent.) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 11 Tf
(Transaction States) Tj
0 -14 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(A transaction moves through the following states during its execution:) Tj
0 -14 Td
(1  Active - The initial state; the transaction is executing.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(2  Partially Committed - After the final statement has executed, but before commit.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(3  Committed - After successful completion; changes are permanently saved.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(4  Failed - When normal execution can no longer proceed (due to a hardware/logical error).) Tj
0 -13 Td
(5  Aborted - After the transaction has been rolled back and the database restored to its prior state.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(6  Terminated - The transaction leaves the system (after commit or abort).) Tj
0 -16 Td
/F1 9.5 Tf
(State flow (conceptual):) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F2 9 Tf
(Active -> Partially Committed -> Committed -> Terminated) Tj
0 -12 Td
(Active/Partially Committed -> Failed -> Aborted -> Terminated) Tj
0 -16 Td
/F1 11 Tf
(Operations on a Transaction) Tj
0 -14 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(* Read(X): Reads the value of data item X from the database into a buffer.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(* Write(X): Writes the value from the buffer back into the database.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(* Commit: Signals successful completion; changes become permanent.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(* Rollback/Abort: Undoes all changes made by the transaction.) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 13 Tf
(2. ACID Properties) Tj
0 -15 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(For a transaction to maintain database consistency and reliability, it must satisfy four key properties: ACID.) Tj
0 -15 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(A - Atomicity: ) Tj
/F2 9.5 Tf
("All or nothing" principle. A transaction is treated as a single, indivisible unit -- either all its operations) Tj
0 -13 Td
(are executed, or none are. Managed using transaction logs and rollback mechanisms.) Tj
0 -15 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(C - Consistency: ) Tj
/F2 9.5 Tf
(A transaction must take the database from one valid (consistent) state to another valid state,) Tj
0 -13 Td
(preserving all integrity constraints (primary keys, foreign keys, triggers).) Tj
0 -15 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(I - Isolation: ) Tj
/F2 9.5 Tf
(Multiple transactions executing concurrently should not interfere with one another. The intermediate) Tj
0 -13 Td
(state of a transaction should not be visible until commit (achieved via locking and scheduling).) Tj
0 -15 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(D - Durability: ) Tj
/F2 9.5 Tf
(Once committed, changes must persist permanently, even during crashes or power failure (WAL logging).) Tj
