0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(Property         | Ensures                             | Mechanism Used) Tj
0 -12 Td
/F2 9 Tf
(------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
0 -14 Td
(Atomicity        | All-or-nothing execution            | Logging, rollback) Tj
0 -13 Td
(Consistency      | Valid state transitions             | Constraints, triggers) Tj
0 -13 Td
(Isolation        | No interference between transactions| Locking, scheduling) Tj
0 -13 Td
(Durability       | Permanence after commit             | Write-Ahead Logging (WAL), checkpoints) Tj
0 -14 Td
(------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
0 -22 Td
/F1 13 Tf
(3. Concurrent Execution & Its Problems) Tj
0 -15 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(Multiple transactions running simultaneously improve system throughput and resource utilization, but if not) Tj
0 -13 Td
(controlled properly, they lead to data inconsistency. Common concurrency anomalies include:) Tj
0 -16 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(a) Lost Update Problem:) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(Two transactions read the same data item and update it, and one update overwrites the other, causing the first to be lost.) Tj
0 -15 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(b) Dirty Read Problem (Temporary Inconsistency):) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(A transaction reads data that has been modified by another transaction that has not yet committed. If T2 aborts, T1 read invalid data.) Tj
0 -15 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(c) Unrepeatable Read Problem:) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(A transaction reads the same data item twice and gets different values because another transaction modified and committed changes.) Tj
0 -15 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(d) Phantom Read Problem:) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(A transaction re-executes a query returning a set of rows and finds that the set has changed (rows inserted/deleted by T2).) Tj
0 -22 Td
/F1 13 Tf
(4. Schedules & Serializability) Tj
0 -15 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(A schedule is the chronological order in which the operations of multiple transactions are executed.) Tj
0 -15 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(Types of Schedules:) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(1  Serial Schedule: Transactions execute one after another with no overlapping -- always consistent, but inefficient.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(2  Concurrent (Non-serial) Schedule: Operations of multiple transactions are interleaved -- improves performance.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(3  Serializable Schedule: A concurrent schedule that produces the same result as some serial execution.) Tj
0 -16 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(Serializability & Recoverability:) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(* Conflict Serializability: Can be transformed into a serial schedule by swapping non-conflicting operations.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(* View Serializability: Schedule is view-equivalent to some serial schedule (same reads, same final writes).) Tj
0 -13 Td
(* Recoverable Schedule: A transaction commits only after all transactions from which it has read have committed.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(* Cascadeless Schedule: A transaction reads only values written by committed transactions -- avoids cascading aborts.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(* Strict Schedule: A transaction neither reads nor writes data until the last writer commits -- safest form.) Tj
ET
endstream
