(5. Concurrency Control Protocols & 6. Deadlocks) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F2 9 Tf
(------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
0 -16 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(Concurrency control ensures concurrent transaction execution leads to a consistent, serializable database state.) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 11 Tf
(5.1 Lock-Based Protocols) Tj
0 -14 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(* Shared Lock (S): Allows a transaction to read a data item; multiple transactions can hold S locks simultaneously.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(* Exclusive Lock (X): Allows both read and write; only one transaction can hold X lock at a time.) Tj
0 -15 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(Two-Phase Locking (2PL) Protocol:) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(1  Growing Phase: The transaction may acquire locks but cannot release any.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(2  Shrinking Phase: The transaction may release locks but cannot acquire any new ones.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(Variants: Strict 2PL (holds exclusive locks until commit/abort); Rigorous 2PL (holds all locks until commit).) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 11 Tf
(5.2 Timestamp-Based Protocols) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(Each transaction is assigned a unique timestamp TS(Ti) upon arrival. Conflicts are resolved ensuring transactions) Tj
0 -13 Td
(are ordered as if executed serially by timestamp order. Thomas' Write Rule allows out-of-order obsolete writes to be skipped.) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 11 Tf
(5.3 Validation (Optimistic) Concurrency Control) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(Assumes conflicts are rare. Executes in three phases: (1) Read Phase (updates local copy), (2) Validation Phase) Tj
0 -13 Td
((checks serializability violations), (3) Write Phase (commits changes to database). Best for low-conflict workloads.) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 11 Tf
(5.4 Multiple Granularity Locking) Tj
0 -13 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(Locks at different hierarchy levels (database -> table -> page -> row) using intention locks (IS, IX) to optimize lock overhead.) Tj
0 -22 Td
/F1 13 Tf
(6. Deadlocks) Tj
0 -15 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(A deadlock occurs when two or more transactions are waiting indefinitely for one another to release locks,) Tj
0 -13 Td
(forming a cycle of dependencies (e.g., T1 holds A, waits for B; T2 holds B, waits for A).) Tj
0 -16 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(Deadlock Handling Approaches:) Tj
0 -14 Td
/F2 9.5 Tf
(1  Deadlock Prevention: Wait-Die scheme (older waits, younger dies) or Wound-Wait scheme (older wounds/aborts younger).) Tj
0 -13 Td
(2  Deadlock Detection: System constructs a Wait-For Graph (WFG); if a cycle is detected, a victim transaction is rolled back.) Tj
0 -13 Td
(3  Deadlock Avoidance: Uses timestamp ordering or resource allocation graphs to ensure safe states before granting locks.) Tj
ET
endstream
